{
  "id": "COMPOUND:450 - PROTEIN:801",
  "verbalization": "Methscopolamine bromide is implicated in the pathogenesis of torsade de pointes, a life-threatening cardiac arrhythmia, through a mechanism that involves the GABA(A) receptor benzodiazepine binding site. This relationship is supported by multiple evidence paths, all of which indicate that Methscopolamine bromide exerts its effects via a common downstream pathway leading to torsade de pointes. The shared effect from torsade de pointes to the GABA(A) receptor binding site suggests a potential role in modulating GABAergic signaling, possibly through interactions with benzodiazepine receptors. The evidence from the three paths, which all link Methscopolamine bromide to torsade de pointes and then to the GABA(A) receptor, collectively supports a mechanistic link between the drug and the receptor site, though the exact nature of the interaction remains unclear. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the GABA(A) receptor binding site implies that the drug may influence GABAergic signaling in a manner that could be relevant to cardiac electrophysiology.",
